   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-statistics",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.12",
    "topic_title": "Setting Up a Test for the Difference Between Two Population Proportions",
    "stem": "Independent random samples of 100 adults from District 1 and 300 adults from District 2 include 40 and 90 adults, respectively, who support a proposed bus route. Each district has more than 10,000 adults. Let p₁ and p₂ be the population proportions of adults who support the route in Districts 1 and 2, respectively. Researchers want to test whether support is greater in District 1. Which hypotheses and pooled sample proportion correctly set up this test?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "H₀: p₁ − p₂ = 0; Hₐ: p₁ − p₂ < 0; pooled proportion = 130/400 = 0.325.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-direction error: p₁ − p₂ < 0 describes less support in District 1, contrary to the research question. Fix: Translate the claim into p₁ > p₂ before writing the difference."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "H₀: p̂₁ − p̂₂ = 0; Hₐ: p̂₁ − p̂₂ > 0; pooled proportion = 130/400 = 0.325.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Statistic-for-parameter error: these hypotheses concern observed sample proportions rather than the unknown population proportions researchers want to compare. Fix: Write hypotheses using population parameters, leaving sample statistics for calculations."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "H₀: p₁ − p₂ = 0; Hₐ: p₁ − p₂ > 0; pooled proportion = (0.40 + 0.30)/2 = 0.35.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Unweighted-average error: averaging the two sample proportions gives unequal-sized samples equal weight. Pooling must count every sampled adult equally. Fix: Add the supporter counts and divide by the combined sample size."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "H₀: p₁ − p₂ = 0; Hₐ: p₁ − p₂ > 0; pooled proportion = 130/400 = 0.325.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Greater support in District 1 means p₁ > p₂, so the alternative is p₁ − p₂ > 0. The null assumes equal population proportions, so estimate that common proportion by pooling all 130 supporters among all 400 sampled adults."
     }
    ]
   },
